    "image": "img1"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The Arsacid rulers were titled “King of Kings”, claiming the inheritance of the Achaemenid Empire."
   },
   {
    "type": "bignumber",
    "fact": "f40",
    "display": "20,000",
    "caption": "With some 20,000 Romans dead, approximately 10,000 captured."
   },
   {
    "type": "closing",
    "text": "Aside from scattered cuneiform tablets, fragmentary ostraca, rock inscriptions, drachma coins."
   }
  ],
  "medium": [
   {
    "type": "title",
    "title": "Parthian Empire",
    "line": "Iranian empire",
    "image": "img1"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The Parthian Empire was a major Iranian political and cultural power centered in ancient Iran from 247 BC to 224 AD."
   },
   {
    "type": "image",
    "image": "img1",
    "caption": "Parthian Empire"
   },
   {
    "type": "bignumber",
    "fact": "f81",
    "display": "400 years",
    "caption": "Nevertheless, for a period of more than 400 years, they succeeded the Parthian realm as Rome’s principal rival."
   },
   {
    "type": "map",
    "map": "m1",
    "label": "Parthian Empire"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "Mithridates I greatly expanded the empire by seizing Media and Mesopotamia from the Seleucids."
   },
   {
    "type": "closing",
    "text": "Parthian artwork is a means of understanding aspects of society and culture that are otherwise absent in textual sources."
   }
  ],
  "deep": [
   {
    "type": "title",
    "title": "Parthian Empire",
    "line": "Iranian empire",
    "image": "img1"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The Parthian Empire, also known as the Arsacid Empire, was a major Iranian political and cultural power centered in ancient Iran from 247 BC to 224 AD."
   },
   {
    "type": "image",
    "image": "img1",
    "caption": "Parthian Empire"
   },
   {
    "type": "bignumber",
    "fact": "f81",
    "display": "400 years",
    "caption": "Nevertheless, for a period of more than 400 years, they succeeded the Parthian realm as Rome’s principal rival."
   },
   {
    "type": "map",
    "map": "m1",
    "label": "Parthian Empire"
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "Before Arsaces I founded the Arsacid dynasty, he was chieftain of the Parni, an ancient Central Asian tribe of Iranian peoples and one of several nomadic tribes within the confederation of the Dahae."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "The latter was a northeastern province, first under the Achaemenid Empire, and then the Seleucid Empire."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "After conquering the region, the Parni adopted Parthian as the official court language, speaking it alongside Middle Persian, Aramaic, Greek, Babylonian, Sogdian and other languages in the multilingual territories they would conquer."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "Some academics debate the ethnic origins of Arsaces I, such as Hashem Kazemi and Mostafa Dehpahlavan, who argue that he was a Parthian native rather than a member of the invading Parni."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "Why the Arsacid court retroactively chose 247 BC as the first year of the Arsacid era is uncertain."
   },
   {
    "type": "sentence",
    "text": "However, Vesta Sarkhosh Curtis asserts that this was simply the year Arsaces was made chief of the Parni tribe."
   },
   {
    "type": "closing",
    "text": "Parthian artwork is a means of understanding aspects of society and culture that are otherwise absent in textual sources."
   }
  ]
 },
